## What was configured

**Mailman** (`/etc/mailman3/mailman.cfg`)
- `site_owner: root@local.edu` (bounce/owner notifications land in a local mailbox)
- `[mta] incoming: mailman.mta.postfix.LMTP`, LMTP on `127.0.0.1:8024`, outgoing SMTP to `localhost:25`
- Debian paths/layout left intact; DB at `/var/lib/mailman3/data/mailman.db`

**List** `reading-group@local.edu`
- Created with owner `root@local.edu`
- `subscription_policy: open` (no owner approval needed), `default_member_action: accept`, `default_nonmember_action: accept`
- Join/leave still require the user to reply to the confirmation message (Mailman's native `-join`/`-leave`/`-confirm` robot addresses)

**Postfix**
- `mydestination = $myhostname, localhost, localhost.localdomain, local.edu` and `myorigin = local.edu`, so `<user>@local.edu` is delivered by `local(8)` into `/var/mail/<user>` (mbox)
- `transport_maps = hash:/var/lib/mailman3/data/postfix_lmtp, regexp:/etc/postfix/mailman_transport.regexp` — the first is Mailman-generated (`reading-group`, `-join`, `-leave`, `-confirm`, `-request`, `-bounces`, `-owner`, ...); the regexp map catches the VERP-style `reading-group-confirm+<cookie>@local.edu` reply addresses
- `local_recipient_maps` includes the Mailman transport map so list addresses aren't rejected as unknown local users
- `postfix_lmtp`/`postfix_domains` and the regexp map are world-readable so the postfix daemon (non-root) can read them

